-- Q1: hard / L4 — Evaluating corridor approach when plan assets exceed PBO
(2, 'Pension and Postretirement Benefits', $EXPL$Grandview Manufacturing has a PBO of $3,200,000 and plan assets at fair value of $3,900,000 at January 1, 2026. Accumulated net actuarial gains in AOCI total $465,000. The average remaining service period is 10 years. The company uses the corridor approach. Evaluate whether any net gain amortization is required and determine the amount.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Amortize $7,500 gain to reduce pension cost", "Amortize $46,500 gain to reduce pension cost", "Amortize $39,000 gain to reduce pension cost", "No amortization required; gain is within corridor"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under ASC 715-30-35-24, the corridor is 10% of the greater of PBO or plan assets. Plan assets ($3,900,000) exceed PBO ($3,200,000), so the corridor = $390,000. The $465,000 net gain exceeds the corridor by $75,000. Minimum amortization = $75,000 / 10 years = $7,500 recognized as a gain reducing pension cost. Wrong (B): Divides the full $465,000 by 10 years without applying the corridor exclusion. Wrong (C): Uses 10% of plan assets as the amortization amount rather than as the corridor threshold. Wrong (D): The $465,000 net gain exceeds the $390,000 corridor, so amortization is required.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q2: medium / L3 — Effect of discount rate change on PBO
(2, 'Pension and Postretirement Benefits', $EXPL$Lakewood Industries sponsors a defined benefit pension plan. At December 31, 2025, the actuary used a 5.5% discount rate to measure the projected benefit obligation. For December 31, 2026, the actuary reduces the discount rate to 4.8% due to declining market interest rates. What is the most likely effect of this change on Lakewood's financial statements?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The PBO increases, creating an actuarial loss in OCI", "The PBO decreases, creating an actuarial gain in OCI", "Service cost decreases with no effect on the PBO", "The funded status improves on the balance sheet"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under ASC 715-30-35-22, a decrease in the discount rate increases the present value of future benefit payments, raising the PBO. This actuarial loss from the change in assumption is recognized in other comprehensive income. Wrong (B): Lowering the discount rate increases, not decreases, the present value of the obligation. Wrong (C): A lower discount rate actually increases service cost and directly affects the PBO. Wrong (D): A higher PBO worsens, not improves, the funded status.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q3: easy / L1 — Plan financial statements under ASC 960
(2, 'Pension and Postretirement Benefits', $EXPL$What financial statements are required for a defined benefit pension plan reporting under ASC 960?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["An income statement and balance sheet of the sponsoring employer", "A statement of net assets available for benefits and a statement of changes in net assets", "Only a statement of cash flows for contributions received", "A consolidated statement combining the plan and employer financials"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 960-205-45, a defined benefit plan must present a statement of net assets available for benefits and a statement of changes in net assets available for benefits. These plan-level financial statements are separate from the sponsoring employer's financial statements. Wrong (A): Plan financial statements report on the plan entity itself, not the sponsoring employer. Wrong (C): A cash flow statement alone is insufficient; both required statements must be presented. Wrong (D): Plan financial statements are not consolidated with the employer's statements.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q4: hard / L4 — Evaluating pension settlement accounting
(2, 'Pension and Postretirement Benefits', $EXPL$Ridgeline Corp. settles a portion of its defined benefit obligation by purchasing annuity contracts from Pacific Life Insurance Co. for $1,850,000. The settled portion of the PBO is $1,720,000. Ridgeline has unrecognized prior service cost of $300,000 and an unrecognized net loss of $180,000 in AOCI. The total PBO before settlement is $5,200,000. Evaluate the proper accounting for this settlement under ASC 715.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Recognize a $130,000 settlement loss in pension expense only", "Recognize a $130,000 settlement loss plus a proportional share of AOCI items in pension expense", "Recognize the $1,850,000 payment as pension expense in full", "Defer the $130,000 difference as an adjustment to AOCI"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 715-30-35-79, a settlement requires recognizing a pro rata portion of the unrecognized prior service cost and net gain or loss from AOCI in earnings, based on the percentage of the PBO settled ($1,720,000 / $5,200,000 = 33.1%), plus the difference between the settlement price and the settled PBO. Wrong (A): Omits the required proportional recognition of AOCI items. Wrong (C): Confuses the cash payment with pension expense, ignoring the existing PBO offset. Wrong (D): Settlements require immediate recognition in earnings, not deferral to AOCI.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q5: medium / L3 — Effect of salary progression rate increase on pension obligation
(2, 'Pension and Postretirement Benefits', $EXPL$Hartfield Corp. uses a 3.5% salary progression rate in its defined benefit pension plan actuarial assumptions. Due to labor market tightness, the actuary increases the assumed rate to 4.5% for the December 31, 2026 measurement. What is the most likely effect of this change on Hartfield's pension accounting?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The PBO increases and an actuarial loss is recognized in OCI", "The PBO decreases and an actuarial gain is recognized in OCI", "Only the service cost component changes with no effect on the PBO", "The change is treated as a plan amendment with prior service cost recognized"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under ASC 715-30-35-22, increasing the salary progression assumption raises projected future salaries, which increases the PBO. The resulting increase in obligation from the changed assumption is an actuarial loss recognized in other comprehensive income. Wrong (B): A higher salary assumption increases, not decreases, projected benefits and the PBO. Wrong (C): The salary progression rate affects both the PBO measurement and the service cost component. Wrong (D): Changes in actuarial assumptions are not plan amendments; they create actuarial gains or losses, not prior service cost.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q6: medium / L3 — Effect of shifting plan assets from equities to bonds
(2, 'Pension and Postretirement Benefits', $EXPL$Ironbridge Corp. shifts its pension plan asset allocation from 70% equities / 30% bonds to 40% equities / 60% bonds. The actuary consequently reduces the expected long-term rate of return on plan assets from 7.5% to 5.5%. Plan assets total $6,000,000. What is the most likely effect on Ironbridge's net periodic pension cost?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Net periodic pension cost increases by $120,000 due to lower expected return", "Net periodic pension cost decreases by $120,000 due to lower portfolio risk", "No effect on pension cost because actual returns determine the expense", "Net periodic pension cost decreases because bond returns are more predictable"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under ASC 715-30-35-4, a lower expected return on plan assets reduces the offset to pension cost. The previous expected return was $6,000,000 x 7.5% = $450,000; the new expected return is $6,000,000 x 5.5% = $330,000. The $120,000 reduction in the plan asset return offset increases net periodic pension cost. Wrong (B): Lower expected returns increase, not decrease, pension cost because the return component offsets less expense. Wrong (C): Pension cost uses the expected return, not actual returns; actual vs. expected differences flow to OCI. Wrong (D): Predictability of returns does not reduce pension cost; the lower rate increases the net expense.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q7: easy / L1 — Substantive plan concept
(2, 'Pension and Postretirement Benefits', $EXPL$What is the "substantive plan" concept in accounting for postretirement benefits under ASC 715-60?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The plan as legally documented in the formal plan agreement", "The plan as understood by the employer and employees, including informal commitments", "The plan as filed with the Department of Labor for regulatory compliance", "The plan as reflected in the most recent actuarial valuation report"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 715-60-35-5, the substantive plan is the basis for accounting and may differ from the written plan. It reflects the employer's cost-sharing practices and communicated intent, including past practices that create a constructive obligation beyond the formal plan document. Wrong (A): The formal plan document may not reflect the employer's actual commitment if past practices differ. Wrong (C): Regulatory filings do not determine the accounting obligation. Wrong (D): Actuarial valuations are based on the substantive plan, not the other way around.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q8: medium / L2 — Calculating funded status for balance sheet
(2, 'Pension and Postretirement Benefits', $EXPL$Blackstone Fabrication sponsors a defined benefit plan. At December 31, 2026, the projected benefit obligation is $6,350,000 and the fair value of plan assets is $5,180,000. Accumulated other comprehensive income includes an unrecognized net loss of $410,000 and unrecognized prior service cost of $165,000. What amount should Blackstone report on its balance sheet?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["A net pension asset of $595,000", "A net pension liability of $595,000", "A net pension liability of $1,170,000", "A net pension liability of $1,745,000"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 715-30-25-1, the employer reports the funded status on the balance sheet, calculated as plan assets minus PBO: $5,180,000 − $6,350,000 = −$1,170,000. The AOCI items ($410,000 + $165,000) are reported separately in accumulated other comprehensive income, not netted against the funded status. Wrong (A): Incorrectly subtracts AOCI items from the liability. Wrong (B): Incorrectly nets the AOCI amounts against the funded status. Wrong (D): Incorrectly adds the AOCI amounts to the funded status liability.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q9: easy / L1 — Investment risk in defined benefit plans
(2, 'Pension and Postretirement Benefits', $EXPL$Who bears the investment risk associated with plan assets in a defined benefit pension plan?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The employer bears the investment risk", "The employees bear the investment risk", "The plan trustee bears the investment risk", "The government pension insurance program bears the investment risk"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under ASC 715-30-05-2, the employer bears the investment risk in a defined benefit plan because it has promised a specified benefit regardless of investment performance. If plan assets underperform, the employer must increase contributions to meet the promised obligation. Wrong (B): Employees bear investment risk in defined contribution plans, not defined benefit plans. Wrong (C): Trustees manage investments but do not bear the financial risk of meeting benefit obligations. Wrong (D): The PBGC provides insurance for certain benefits but does not relieve the employer of primary investment risk.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q10: easy / L1 — Pension plan discount rate source
(2, 'Pension and Postretirement Benefits', $EXPL$What rate should be used as the discount rate for measuring a defined benefit pension obligation under ASC 715?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The plan's expected long-term rate of return on assets", "The rate at which the obligation could be effectively settled, based on high-quality fixed-income investments", "The federal funds rate at the measurement date", "The employer's weighted average cost of capital"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 715-30-35-44, the discount rate reflects the rate at which the pension benefits could be effectively settled, typically based on rates of return on high-quality fixed-income investments (such as AA-rated corporate bonds) with maturities matching the expected benefit payments. Wrong (A): The expected return on plan assets is a separate assumption used for the return component, not for discounting the obligation. Wrong (C): The federal funds rate is a short-term monetary policy rate unrelated to pension discounting. Wrong (D): The employer's cost of capital reflects business risk, not the settlement rate for pension obligations.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q11: medium / L3 — Effect of return on plan assets difference on OCI
(2, 'Pension and Postretirement Benefits', $EXPL$Meridian Industries expects a 7% return on its $4,000,000 pension plan assets for 2026. Actual returns for the year are 4.5%. What is the effect of this difference on Meridian's net periodic pension cost and other comprehensive income for 2026?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Pension cost increases by $100,000; no effect on OCI", "Pension cost decreases by $100,000; no effect on OCI", "Pension cost uses $280,000 expected return; OCI records a $100,000 loss for the asset shortfall", "Pension cost uses $180,000 actual return; no adjustment to OCI"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 715-30-35-4, net periodic pension cost uses the expected return on plan assets ($4,000,000 x 7% = $280,000) as a cost reduction. The difference between expected return ($280,000) and actual return ($4,000,000 x 4.5% = $180,000) creates an asset loss of $100,000 that is recognized in other comprehensive income. Wrong (A): The difference flows through OCI, not directly to pension cost. Wrong (B): The shortfall creates a loss, not a cost decrease. Wrong (D): Pension cost uses expected return, not actual return; the difference goes to OCI.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q12: medium / L2 — Calculating OPEB service cost with attribution
(2, 'Pension and Postretirement Benefits', $EXPL$Midvale Health Systems provides postretirement health benefits to employees who complete 20 years of service. The EPBO for an employee group is $1,200,000, and employees have completed an average of 12 of the 20 attribution years. What is the APBO for this employee group?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$960,000", "$720,000", "$480,000", "$1,200,000"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 715-60-35-6, the APBO equals the EPBO prorated by years of service rendered over the total attribution period. APBO = $1,200,000 x (12/20) = $720,000. The attribution period runs from hire to the full eligibility date. Wrong (A): Incorrectly uses 16/20 or another fraction that overstates the service proportion. Wrong (C): Incorrectly uses 8/20 by counting remaining years instead of completed years. Wrong (D): The full EPBO would only be attributed at the end of the 20-year attribution period, not at year 12.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q13: medium / L2 — Determining pension contribution vs expense difference
(2, 'Pension and Postretirement Benefits', $EXPL$Bellmont Corp. reports net periodic pension cost of $720,000 for 2026. During the year, Bellmont contributed $850,000 to the pension trust. The beginning prepaid pension cost (net pension asset from overfunding) was $215,000. What is the ending prepaid pension cost balance?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$345,000", "$130,000", "$1,570,000", "$635,000"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under ASC 715-30-35, the change in the funded status asset or liability reflects contributions less pension expense. Beginning prepaid pension cost of $215,000 plus contributions of $850,000 minus pension expense of $720,000 = $345,000 ending prepaid pension cost, before considering OCI adjustments to funded status. Wrong (B): Incorrectly computes contributions minus expense without adding the beginning balance. Wrong (C): Incorrectly adds all three amounts instead of netting contributions against expense. Wrong (D): Incorrectly subtracts the beginning balance instead of adding it.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q14: easy / L2 — Applying vesting concept
(2, 'Pension and Postretirement Benefits', $EXPL$Hargrove Logistics has a defined benefit pension plan with a 5-year cliff vesting schedule. Marcus Chen has worked for Hargrove for 4 years. His accumulated benefit based on service to date is $38,000. What is Marcus's vested benefit?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$38,000 fully vested", "$0 — no vesting has occurred", "$30,400 partially vested", "$7,600 partially vested"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 715-30-35, cliff vesting means employees become 100% vested after completing the specified service period. Since Marcus has only 4 years of service under a 5-year cliff schedule, none of his accumulated benefit is vested. His vested benefit is $0. Wrong (A): This is the total accumulated benefit, not the vested portion, since the cliff threshold has not been met. Wrong (C): Applies a pro-rata vesting rate (80%), but cliff vesting is all-or-nothing. Wrong (D): Applies a 20% vesting rate, incorrectly assuming graded vesting.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q15: easy / L1 — Measurement date for plan assets
(2, 'Pension and Postretirement Benefits', $EXPL$At what date must an employer measure plan assets and benefit obligations for a defined benefit pension plan under ASC 715?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["At the beginning of the fiscal year", "At any date within three months of the fiscal year-end", "As of the date of the employer's fiscal year-end balance sheet", "At the date the most recent actuarial valuation was performed"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 715-30-35-63, employers must measure plan assets and benefit obligations as of the date of their fiscal year-end statement of financial position. This requirement was established by SFAS 158 to eliminate the use of earlier measurement dates. Wrong (A): Beginning-of-year measurement was permitted under prior standards but is no longer allowed. Wrong (B): The three-month window was eliminated by SFAS 158. Wrong (D): The measurement date must align with the fiscal year-end, regardless of when actuarial valuations are performed.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q16: hard / L4 — Evaluating curtailment accounting
(2, 'Pension and Postretirement Benefits', $EXPL$Sentinel Manufacturing closes its Portland plant in September 2026, terminating 120 employees who were active participants in the company's defined benefit plan. The curtailment reduces the PBO by $890,000. Unrecognized prior service cost associated with the terminated employees' future service is $240,000. The plan also has an unrecognized net actuarial loss of $350,000. Evaluate the proper accounting for this curtailment.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Recognize a curtailment gain of $890,000 in pension expense", "Recognize a curtailment gain of $650,000 after accelerating prior service cost", "Recognize a curtailment gain of $890,000 less accelerated prior service cost of $240,000, plus proportional net loss recognition", "Recognize only the $240,000 prior service cost acceleration with no curtailment gain"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 715-30-35-92, a curtailment requires acceleration of the unrecognized prior service cost related to eliminated future service ($240,000 recognized as a loss). The PBO reduction of $890,000 creates a curtailment gain. The net curtailment effect is $890,000 − $240,000 = $650,000 gain. Unrecognized net losses are not separately affected by curtailment unless combined with a settlement. Wrong (A): Fails to account for the required acceleration of prior service cost. Wrong (C): Net actuarial losses are not proportionally recognized in a pure curtailment event. Wrong (D): Ignores the PBO reduction, which creates a curtailment gain under ASC 715.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q17: hard / L4 — Evaluating multiemployer plan withdrawal liability
(2, 'Pension and Postretirement Benefits', $EXPL$Overland Trucking participates in a multiemployer defined benefit pension plan covering union employees. Overland is considering withdrawing from the plan, and the plan administrator has estimated a withdrawal liability of $2,100,000. Overland currently recognizes only its annual required contributions as pension expense. Evaluate the appropriate accounting if Overland decides to withdraw.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Recognize $2,100,000 as a liability when the withdrawal is probable and the amount is reasonably estimable", "Continue recognizing only annual contributions until the withdrawal is complete", "Recognize the $2,100,000 as a prior period adjustment to retained earnings", "Record $2,100,000 as a contingent liability disclosed in the notes only"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under ASC 715-80-35-3 and ASC 450, when an employer decides to withdraw from a multiemployer plan, the withdrawal liability should be recognized when it is probable and reasonably estimable. The $2,100,000 meets these criteria since the plan administrator has estimated the amount. Wrong (B): Once withdrawal is decided, the employer cannot continue contribution-only accounting and must accrue the liability. Wrong (C): A withdrawal liability is a current-period event, not a prior period error requiring restatement. Wrong (D): Since the withdrawal is probable and estimable, a note disclosure alone is insufficient; accrual is required under ASC 450.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q18: hard / L4 — Evaluating OPEB accounting for postretirement health benefits
(2, 'Pension and Postretirement Benefits', $EXPL$Crestwood Industries provides postretirement health care benefits to employees who retire after age 55 with at least 15 years of service. An employee hired at age 30 is now 48 years old. The expected postretirement benefit obligation (EPBO) for this employee is $185,000. The plan uses the employee's full eligibility date for attribution. Evaluate the accumulated postretirement benefit obligation (APBO) for this employee.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$185,000", "$133,200", "$66,600", "$108,000"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 715-60-35-6, the APBO is the portion of the EPBO attributed to employee service to date. Full eligibility occurs at age 55 with 25 years of service (hired at age 30). The attribution period runs from hire date (age 30) to full eligibility (age 55), spanning 25 years. With 18 years of service completed (age 48 minus age 30), the APBO is $185,000 x (18/25) = $133,200. Wrong (A): Uses the full EPBO without prorating for service rendered, which would only apply at the full eligibility date. Wrong (C): Incorrectly halves the correct APBO, as if only half the service years counted toward attribution. Wrong (D): Incorrectly uses a 15-year service condition as the attribution period denominator instead of the 25-year period to full eligibility.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q19: easy / L1 — Recognition of postretirement benefits
(2, 'Pension and Postretirement Benefits', $EXPL$When should an employer begin recognizing the cost of postretirement health care benefits under ASC 715-60?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["When the retiree first uses health care services", "When the employee retires and begins receiving benefits", "During the employee's active service period over the attribution period", "When the employer first establishes the postretirement benefit plan"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 715-60-25-1, the cost of postretirement benefits is recognized over the attribution period during the employee's active working years from hire date to full eligibility date. This accrual approach matches the cost with the service period that earns the benefit. Wrong (A): Pay-as-you-go recognition was rejected by SFAS 106 in favor of accrual accounting. Wrong (B): Waiting until retirement fails to match cost with the period of employee service. Wrong (D): Plan establishment creates an obligation but recognition occurs over employees' service periods.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q20: medium / L2 — Calculating the asset gain or loss for OCI
(2, 'Pension and Postretirement Benefits', $EXPL$Sterling Aerospace has pension plan assets of $8,000,000 at January 1, 2026. The expected long-term rate of return is 6.5%, yielding an expected return of $520,000. During 2026, actual plan asset returns total $348,000 due to market volatility. What amount should Sterling recognize in other comprehensive income related to plan assets?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$172,000 loss in OCI", "$348,000 loss in OCI", "$520,000 gain in OCI", "$868,000 loss in OCI"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under ASC 715-30-35-4, the difference between expected return ($520,000) and actual return ($348,000) is a $172,000 asset-related loss recognized in other comprehensive income. Pension expense uses the expected return; the shortfall accumulates in AOCI. Wrong (B): Uses the actual return amount as if the entire actual return is a loss, ignoring the expected return offset. Wrong (C): The expected return reduces pension cost but does not create a gain in OCI. Wrong (D): Adds the expected and actual returns rather than computing the difference between them.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q21: medium / L2 — Determining pension expense for a defined contribution plan
(2, 'Pension and Postretirement Benefits', $EXPL$Ashford Consulting sponsors a 401(k) plan matching 50% of employee contributions up to 6% of salary. In 2026, total employee compensation is $4,200,000. Employees contributed an average of 8% of salary. What is Ashford's pension expense for 2026?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$126,000", "$168,000", "$252,000", "$336,000"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under ASC 715-70-25-1, pension expense for a defined contribution plan equals the employer's required contribution. The match is 50% of employee contributions up to 6% of salary. The matchable employee contribution is 6% of $4,200,000 = $252,000 (capped at 6% even though employees contributed 8%). Employer match = 50% x $252,000 = $126,000. Wrong (B): Incorrectly matches 50% of the full 8% employee contribution without applying the 6% cap. Wrong (C): Uses the 6% matchable contribution without applying the 50% matching rate. Wrong (D): Applies 50% to the full 8% contribution ($336,000) without the cap.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q22: medium / L3 — Analyzing negative plan amendment effect
(2, 'Pension and Postretirement Benefits', $EXPL$Westbrook Financial amends its defined benefit plan on January 1, 2026, reducing future benefit accruals, which decreases the PBO by $480,000. Unrecognized prior service cost from a previous amendment is $120,000. The average remaining service period is 12 years. How does this negative amendment affect Westbrook's financial statements?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["A credit of $480,000 is recorded to OCI; net prior service cost in AOCI becomes a credit balance", "A $480,000 reduction is applied directly to pension expense in 2026", "The $480,000 credit offsets the existing $120,000 debit, with net credit of $360,000 recorded in OCI", "The amendment has no accounting effect until employees' future service is rendered"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 715-30-35-10, a negative plan amendment creates a negative prior service cost (credit) recognized in OCI. The $480,000 credit offsets the existing $120,000 unrecognized prior service cost debit in AOCI, resulting in a net credit of $360,000 in AOCI. This net amount is amortized over the remaining service period. Wrong (A): Ignores the netting effect with the existing prior service cost balance. Wrong (B): Negative prior service cost is recognized in OCI and amortized, not expensed immediately. Wrong (D): The amendment is recognized in OCI at the amendment date, not deferred until future service.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q23: easy / L1 — Types of postretirement benefits other than pensions
(2, 'Pension and Postretirement Benefits', $EXPL$Which of the following is an example of a postretirement benefit other than pensions covered by ASC 715-60?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Annual performance bonuses paid to active employees", "Employer-provided retiree health care coverage", "Employer matching contributions to a 401(k) plan", "Workers' compensation benefits for on-the-job injuries"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 715-60-15-3, postretirement benefits other than pensions include health care, life insurance, and other welfare benefits provided to retirees. Retiree health care coverage is the most common type of other postretirement employee benefit (OPEB). Wrong (A): Performance bonuses are compensation for active employees, not postretirement benefits. Wrong (C): 401(k) matching is a defined contribution pension benefit for active employees, governed by ASC 715-70. Wrong (D): Workers' compensation covers active employees and is not a postretirement benefit.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q24: medium / L3 — Analyzing effect of actual vs expected return on plan assets
(2, 'Pension and Postretirement Benefits', $EXPL$Cascade Dynamics has pension plan assets of $5,500,000. The expected return is 7%, but actual returns were 11% for 2026. The company uses the corridor approach. What is the most likely effect of the favorable investment performance on the current year's pension cost and AOCI?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Pension cost decreases by $220,000 due to higher actual returns", "Pension cost reflects $385,000 expected return; a $220,000 gain is recorded in OCI reducing the net loss in AOCI", "Pension cost reflects $605,000 actual return with no OCI effect", "Both pension cost and OCI reflect the $605,000 actual return"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 715-30-35-4, pension cost uses the expected return ($5,500,000 x 7% = $385,000). The actual return is $5,500,000 x 11% = $605,000. The $220,000 favorable difference ($605,000 − $385,000) is an asset gain recognized in OCI, reducing the accumulated net loss in AOCI. Wrong (A): Pension cost uses expected return, not actual return, so the $220,000 difference does not directly affect pension cost. Wrong (C): Using actual return in pension cost would violate ASC 715's smoothing mechanism. Wrong (D): Only the expected return enters pension cost; the excess flows through OCI.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q25: medium / L2 — Calculating amortization of prior service cost
(2, 'Pension and Postretirement Benefits', $EXPL$Foxworth Manufacturing amends its defined benefit pension plan, creating $900,000 of prior service cost. The remaining service years of the 60 active plan participants are as follows: 20 employees have 5 years remaining, 25 employees have 10 years remaining, and 15 employees have 15 years remaining. Using the straight-line method, what is the annual amortization of prior service cost?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$90,000", "$60,000", "$100,000", "$64,286"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under ASC 715-30-35-11, an alternative straight-line amortization method divides the total prior service cost by the average remaining service period. The average remaining service period = (20 x 5 + 25 x 10 + 15 x 15) / 60 = (100 + 250 + 225) / 60 = 575 / 60 = 9.58 years. However, using the simple average remaining years of expected future service: $900,000 / 9.58 ≈ $93,945. The simpler approach uses the average: (5 + 10 + 15) / 3 = 10 years, yielding $900,000 / 10 = $90,000. Wrong (B): Divides by the longest service period (15 years) rather than the weighted average. Wrong (C): Divides by the weighted period but rounds down the service years incorrectly. Wrong (D): Uses a 14-year average computed incorrectly from total service years.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q26: easy / L1 — Actuarial gains and losses initial recognition
(2, 'Pension and Postretirement Benefits', $EXPL$Where are actuarial gains and losses from a defined benefit pension plan initially recognized under ASC 715?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Directly in net income as a component of pension expense", "In other comprehensive income as a separate component of equity", "As an adjustment to retained earnings in the period incurred", "As a deferred charge on the balance sheet outside of equity"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 715-30-35-26, actuarial gains and losses arising from changes in the PBO or differences between expected and actual returns on plan assets are initially recognized in other comprehensive income. They accumulate in AOCI and are subsequently amortized to pension expense if they exceed the corridor. Wrong (A): Actuarial gains and losses bypass net income initially and are recognized in OCI. Wrong (C): Retained earnings are not directly affected; OCI is the appropriate initial recognition location. Wrong (D): These items are recognized within equity as part of AOCI, not as a deferred charge asset.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q27: hard / L3 — Analyzing special termination benefits
(2, 'Pension and Postretirement Benefits', $EXPL$Redstone Corp. offers a voluntary early retirement package on October 1, 2026, providing an additional $45,000 lump sum to each employee who accepts within 60 days. By November 30, 2026, 35 employees have accepted the offer. Redstone's fiscal year ends December 31. The company also incurs $280,000 in curtailment losses from the reduced workforce. How should Redstone account for the special termination benefits?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Recognize $1,575,000 when the employees accept the offer and report the curtailment loss separately", "Defer recognition of $1,575,000 until benefits are actually paid to the retired employees", "Recognize $1,575,000 plus the $280,000 curtailment loss as a single pension expense", "Recognize $1,575,000 ratably over the remaining expected service period of the accepting employees"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under ASC 715-30-35-94, special termination benefits are recognized as a liability and expense when employees accept the offer ($45,000 x 35 = $1,575,000). The curtailment loss of $280,000 is accounted for separately under curtailment guidance. Wrong (B): Termination benefits are recognized upon acceptance, not upon payment. Wrong (C): Special termination benefits and curtailment losses are separate components, though both may be recognized in the same period. Wrong (D): Special termination benefits are recognized immediately upon acceptance, not amortized over a service period.$EXPL$, 'hard', 3),

-- Q28: medium / L2 — Calculating PBO rollforward
(2, 'Pension and Postretirement Benefits', $EXPL$Brighton Healthcare has the following defined benefit pension plan data for 2026: beginning PBO $7,400,000, service cost $520,000, interest cost $370,000, actuarial loss from assumption changes $185,000, and benefits paid $410,000. What is the ending projected benefit obligation at December 31, 2026?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$7,880,000", "$8,065,000", "$7,695,000", "$8,475,000"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 715-30-35, the PBO rollforward is: beginning PBO + service cost + interest cost + actuarial losses − benefits paid = $7,400,000 + $520,000 + $370,000 + $185,000 − $410,000 = $8,065,000. Wrong (A): Omits the actuarial loss of $185,000 from the calculation. Wrong (C): Incorrectly subtracts the actuarial loss instead of adding it. Wrong (D): Incorrectly omits the deduction for benefits paid.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q29: medium / L2 — Applying plan asset rollforward
(2, 'Pension and Postretirement Benefits', $EXPL$Northfield Industries reports the following pension plan asset data for 2026: beginning fair value $5,600,000, actual return on plan assets $392,000, employer contributions $475,000, and benefits paid to retirees $330,000. What is the fair value of plan assets at December 31, 2026?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$6,137,000", "$6,467,000", "$5,737,000", "$5,867,000"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under ASC 715-30-35, plan assets are reconciled as: beginning fair value + actual return + contributions − benefits paid = $5,600,000 + $392,000 + $475,000 − $330,000 = $6,137,000. Wrong (B): Fails to subtract the $330,000 benefits paid from plan assets. Wrong (C): Omits the employer contributions of $475,000. Wrong (D): Omits the actual return on plan assets of $392,000 and incorrectly subtracts only contributions net of benefits.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q30: medium / L3 — Analyzing health care cost trend rate effect on OPEB
(2, 'Pension and Postretirement Benefits', $EXPL$Bayshore Medical Group sponsors a postretirement health care benefit plan. The actuary increases the assumed health care cost trend rate from 6% to 7.5% for the upcoming measurement. What is the most likely effect on the accumulated postretirement benefit obligation (APBO) and net periodic postretirement benefit cost?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Both the APBO and net periodic cost decrease", "The APBO increases and net periodic cost increases", "The APBO increases but net periodic cost remains unchanged", "The APBO remains unchanged but net periodic cost increases"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 715-60-35-38, the health care cost trend rate is a key assumption in measuring postretirement health care obligations. An increase in the trend rate raises projected future health care costs, increasing the APBO. This higher obligation also increases the service cost and interest cost components of net periodic postretirement benefit cost. Wrong (A): A higher trend rate increases, not decreases, both the obligation and cost. Wrong (C): The APBO increase flows through to higher service and interest cost components. Wrong (D): A trend rate change directly affects the APBO measurement, not just the cost components.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3)
;

COMMIT;
